{"authored" [{"question" "q-74716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=-3-4\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930486/state.json" "file_sha256" "9186a80455cc68616a01432300a59c7e5bf3759d89dd60e1f6e0e48abb1eb896" "basis" 320 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930486/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-74716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930486/assets/7eff12ed4bca73dc27f834b0740b5b2235440250e11427f911c8fc29c99fa1d7.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the third quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\pi <arg⁡(z)<\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ If $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha  & =arctan⁡(\\frac{4}{3})≈0.927\\,30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)≈\\pi +0.927\\,30≈4.07\n$$\n\nto two decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930486/state.json" "file_sha256" "9186a80455cc68616a01432300a59c7e5bf3759d89dd60e1f6e0e48abb1eb896" "basis" 320 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930486/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-74716" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930486/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "59830984d11c84f433473922c52bfca2dd9f8745ba91fa00a054f7687658f69a" "basis" 320 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/referenc